[38;5;231m⏺[39m The layout question is visual, so I've opened a browser tab with two wireframes:
  [94m]8;id=gm19ey;http://localhost:51539/?key=4346848c8305b6711c339252a89640151efae660e8bee166ea358292b99dcf1c\http://localhost:51539/?key=4346848c8305b6711c339252a89640151efae660e8bee166ea358292b99dcf1c[39m]8;;\

  The companion uses a lot of tokens, so say so if you'd rather keep this in the terminal. It saves its files under
  [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m in the repo, which isn't ignored yet because there's no [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m. I won't commit those files. Whether
  to add a [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m entry is up to you.

  [1mFourth question: where the filters sit.[0m

  The choice is between always showing all nine type checkboxes and keeping them behind a click.

  - [1mA. Always-visible panel above the table.[0m No hidden state and the least JavaScript, because it's plain form controls.
    It costs about a third of the first screen before any rows show, more on a phone at 320px. Easy to collapse into B
    later.
  - [1mB. Compact row with event types in a popover.[0m Active filters show as removable tags. The table starts higher on the
    page. The cost is more JavaScript (opening and closing, moving focus, Escape to close, clicking outside), more lines
    toward the 300 limit, and more to check against 02 and 04 for accessibility.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m A. It's smaller, holds up under keyboard and screen reader use, and fits the size budget. The
  failed sign-ins shortcut keeps the common case to one click either way.

  Have a look, click a card if you like, and tell me here which one you prefer.

